    testable under Node. Quota errors or private-mode failures degrade to memory-only rather than throwing.

  Levels are [38;5;153mdebug < info < warn < error[39m, defaulting to [38;5;153minfo[39m. Override for a live debugging session via [38;5;153m?log=debug[39m or
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage.setItem("applog.level", "debug")[39m, read once at init.

  [1mExport hook[0m, installed in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m: [38;5;153mwindow.__appLog = { dump(), copy(), clear() }[39m — [38;5;153mdump()[39m returns the buffer as pretty
  JSON, [38;5;153mcopy()[39m writes it to the clipboard, [38;5;153mclear()[39m wipes buffer and storage. That's the thing you ask a user to run when
  they report a problem.

  [1mSection 5 — What gets instrumented, and testing[0m

  The global handlers are the real production win and are entirely absent today: [38;5;153mwindow.addEventListener("error")[39m and
  [38;5;153m"unhandledrejection"[39m both log at [38;5;153merror[39m with message and stack. A thrown exception in the submit handler currently
  vanishes; after this it lands in the buffer.

  The submit path logs [38;5;153mlogin.attempt[39m → [38;5;153mlogin.result[39m, with the [38;5;153mlogin()[39m call wrapped so a throw becomes [38;5;153mlogin.error[39m rather
  than an unhandled rejection. [38;5;153mapp.start[39m fires once at init with the user agent (browser/version is usually the first
  question a production bug raises).

  Tests ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m, no DOM, fake clock and fake storage injected): undeclared context keys are dropped and marked; an
  unregistered event drops all context; level filtering; ring-buffer eviction at capacity; hash is stable and never
  contains the input substring; a throwing sink doesn't propagate and is disabled after one failure; storage quota
  failure degrades to memory-only.

  I'll write these test-first — the core is pure functions over injected dependencies, so TDD is cheap here.

  ---

  Does each section look right? In particular I'd like a read on [1mSection 3[0m — the hashing is deliberately
  weak-but-honest, and it's the one place where I've traded a security property for the zero-dependency constraint. If
  any section needs changing, tell me which; otherwise say the word and I'll write this up as a spec in
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-17-browser-logging-design.md[39m (uncommitted), run it through review, and then move to the
  implementation plan.

